A||||8||WisconsinGT||Timing chain help||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||03-03-2001||03:15 PM||||Well, it is time for me to embark on the timeless art of replacing my timing chain. I wanted to ask what else I should change wile I am in that area? I mean, is it just the chain or should I be concerned about other things that relate to the chain? I will be working on an 88GT 2.8 engine. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||03-03-2001||03:49 PM||||You do mean chain and sprocket set correct. they should always be replaced as a set.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||03-03-2001||04:33 PM||||Cloyes makes a double roller timing chain I just found out.  P/N 9-3137.  Can't wait to get mine.  <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fieropage/af.bmp"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||03-04-2001||10:49 PM||||Don't forget the front crank seal.  You should probably sleeve the balancer as well, or you may end up with an oil leak like I did.<BR>Good time to flush and fill the cooling system, since you have to drain it anyway.  New water pump is also usually &lt;$30 if it is at all suspect.  Good time for new belts, too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||03-05-2001||07:12 PM||||I always say this when someone mentions doing the timing chain.  So I'll say it again...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>The front cover + water pump can be removed as a unit, which saves some scraping and gasket.  Of course, if you need to replace the water pump anyway, never mind.       <P>Also a good time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> to clean and paint your timing mark while the damper's off. Mark the widest slot on the damper (there are 3 of them).  Have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||WisconsinGT||03-05-2001||08:39 PM||||Thanks all! I was hoping I could get away with only taking off the front cover. Is there any advantage getting a double roller timing chain? What does sleeve the balancer mean? Speaking off the balancer, should I get a new one? Can they go bad? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||03-06-2001||03:08 PM||||Double roller chain =&gt; more cost; but I would <B>guess</B> longer life (?)<P>I think sleeving the balancer means to put a cylindrical sleeve on the outside surface of the hub of the balancer, where the crank seal contacts - to provide a smooth surface, and prevent seal wear + leaks.  Probably necessary only if that surface shows signs of wear.<P>Balancer can go bad, if the rubber coupling (the rubber part that connects the hub to the outer part) gets deteriorated or even shears off.  Look at the condition of the rubber and decide.   ||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||03-06-2001||09:31 PM||||When I did my chain and gear set, I didn't sleeve the balancer.  I replaced the seal, but the original had already worn a slight groove in the balancer.  The new seal did not exactly match the groove, so it leaked oil.  So for 8 years, I lived with a perpetually  squealing belt.  I replaced it about every six months, and just assumed it was water or anti-freeze getting on it.<BR>The sleeve cost $8 at Advance Auto (Fel-Pro brand) and it is super-thin stainless steel, and comes with loctite to hold it onto the balancer.  You need to polish the balancer shaft with 400 grit sandpaper prior to installing the sleeve.<BR>So far it has been over a year, and no leaks or squealing at all.||||reg||1
Z||000008||PJ||03-06-2001||10:09 PM||||Don't forget to change the chain dampner along with the chain and sprockets as long as you're in there. Also be wary of rebuilt water pumps. I've taken apart "brand new" rebuilts and they're not pretty inside. Get a new Delco if you need a water pump.||||reg||1
